<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derry</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Londonderry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derry, officially Londonderry, is a city on the River Foyle in the north-west of Northern Ireland. It grew from a sixth-century monastery associated with Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and was rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> companies during the Plantation of Ulster, its walls being completed in 1619. Its Protestant defenders withstood a siege by the army of James II for 105 days in 1689.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derry.jpg" alt="Derry" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aaronwarduk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foyle_Bridge_Derry_at_Dusk_Oblique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
